  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nSurface analysis this morning shows a broad Bermuda high pressure \nsystem in place, providing warm, southerly gulf flow to Indiana and \nthe Ohio Valley. Remnants of the convective cluster over MO/IL and \nIndiana continue to weaken and drift east with warming tops. This \nwas providing a large mid level cloud deck across the region. Radar \nshows some showers still waning near the Wabash River, but some new \ndevelopment was noted over NE Indiana, where a bit more sun and \nheating was found. Aloft a broad, shallow trough was in place east \nof the Rockies across the northern CONUS, with an axis near the \nMississippi River. Dew points across Central Indiana were in the 60s.\n\nTonight...\n\nAlthough some convective development will be possible late this \nafternoon due to diurnal heating, cloud cover has limited this \nsomewhat. Overall, the trend for convection overnight will be \ndiminishing, leading to partly to mostly cloudy skies overnight. \nAlthough our area will remain mainly under the influence of the \nsurface high pressure, another short wave of forcing should be \nworking through the upper midwest overnight, bringing more mid cloud \nfor late tonight and Friday. Forecast soundings show a non saturated \nbut moist column in place over Indiana with pwats over 1.7 inches. \nThus another warm and humid night will be expected with clouds in \nthe area and dew points in the upper 60s.\n\nFriday and Friday Night...\n\nThe upper pattern will persist with weak troughing remaining in \nplace across Indiana and Illinois. Models hint at another short wave \npassing on Friday Night. Meanwhile, Forecast soundings again \nsuggest a favorable situation for diurnal convection with favorable \nCAPE and and high pwats. HRRR suggests late afternoon and evening \nconvection once again, thus given these favorable ingredients, pops \nwill continue to be needed, with the best window from 21Z-01Z. There \nwill be little change in the air mass as the surface high continues \nto keep mainly warm and humid southerly flow in place, resulting in \nhighs in the middle 80s and lows in the upper 60s.\n\nSaturday and Saturday Night...\n\nIndiana will again start the day with the warm and humid airmass in \nplace. Again, depending upon the evolution of these convective \nsystems, convective debris could be lingering as well as any storm \nremnants. Another short wave is suggested to pass within the NW flow \naloft on Saturday, but confidence on timing these weak systems is \nlow. The air mass remains favorable for convection with high \ndewpoints and some instability in place, but much more mid level \ndrying is in place at that time. There does appear to be some \nagreement toward subsidence and drying on Saturday Night in the wake \nof this wave, thus will trend the overall day toward dry weather \nlate in the day and on Saturday Night.\n\nSunday through Thursday...\n\nLittle overall change in the upper pattern is expected through this \nperiod. Models suggest broad, shallow troughing in place across the \nnorthern CONUS east of the Rockies. This will lead to continued NW \nflow aloft with several embedded short waves poised to pass across \nIndiana within the flow aloft. Confidence in any of these waves is \nlow at this time. However models do suggest a weak passing frontal \nboundary on Tuesday and Thursday. Given this, best chances for \nprecip will likely be on these days.\n\nForecast soundings through the week suggest steep lapse rates \npresent with sufficient instability to support convection. Thus this \nin combination with the expected passing waves will necessitate pops \nin each day of the forecast. Temperatures look remain above normal \ngiven the warm and humid southerly surface flow in place.",
